
This directory is intended for project specific (private) libraries.
PlatformIO will compile them to static libraries and link into the executable file.

The source code of each library should be placed in a separate directory
("lib/your_library_name/[Code]").

For example, see the structure of the following example libraries `Foo` and `Bar`:

|--lib
|  |
|  |--Bar
|  |  |--docs
|  |  |--examples
|  |  |--src
|  |     |- Bar.c
|  |     |- Bar.h
|  |  |- library.json (optional. for custom build options, etc) https://docs.platformio.org/page/librarymanager/config.html
|  |
|  |--Foo
|  |  |- Foo.c
|  |  |- Foo.h
|  |
|  |- README --> THIS FILE
|
|- platformio.ini
|--src
   |- main.c

Example contents of `src/main.c` using Foo and Bar:
```
#include <Foo.h>
#include <Bar.h>

int main (void)
{
  ...
}

```

The PlatformIO Library Dependency Finder will find automatically dependent
libraries by scanning project source files.

More information about PlatformIO Library Dependency Finder
- https://docs.platformio.org/page/librarymanager/ldf.html


ESP32 Homework – Dual LED PWM and OLED Display

Author: Misha Rizwan

Project Overview

This project uses an ESP32 DevKit V1 to control two LEDs using Pulse Width Modulation (PWM). Task 1 implements LED fading at different PWM frequencies. Task 2 extends the same project by adding an SSD1306 OLED display to show LED brightness percentages and PWM frequencies in real time.

Hardware Components

* ESP32 DevKit V1
* Red LED
* Green LED
* Two 220-ohm resistors
* SSD1306 OLED display (128 × 64)
* Jumper wires
* Wokwi simulator

Task 1: Dual LED PWM

Objective

To control two LEDs independently using PWM signals with different frequencies and create a smooth fading effect.

Connections

Component	ESP32 Pin
Red LED anode through 220Ω resistor	GPIO 25
Green LED anode through 220Ω resistor	GPIO 26
Both LED cathodes	GND

PWM Configuration

* Red LED frequency: 1000 Hz
* Green LED frequency: 5000 Hz
* PWM resolution: 10 bits
* Maximum duty cycle value: 1023
* Fade update interval: 15 milliseconds

Working

Both LEDs gradually increase and decrease their brightness. Each LED uses a different PWM frequency and fading step. The Serial Monitor displays the brightness percentage and frequency of both LEDs.

Task 2: Dual LED PWM with OLED Display

Objective

To extend Task 1 by adding an SSD1306 OLED display that shows the brightness and PWM frequency of both LEDs.

Additional Connections

OLED Pin	ESP32 Pin
VCC	3V3
GND	GND
SDA	GPIO 21
SCL	GPIO 22

Display Information

The OLED displays:

* Red LED brightness percentage
* Red LED PWM frequency: 1000 Hz
* Green LED brightness percentage
* Green LED PWM frequency: 5000 Hz

Working

The ESP32 controls both LEDs using PWM and continuously updates their brightness. The OLED refreshes every 100 milliseconds to display the current brightness percentages and frequencies. The Serial Monitor also displays the PWM information.

Software Libraries

* Arduino framework
* Adafruit SSD1306
* Adafruit GFX Library
* Wire library

Software Configuration

The project is configured using PlatformIO in platformio.ini. The ESP32 Arduino core version determines which LEDC PWM functions are used.

Simulation and Testing

The project can be simulated in Wokwi. Test both tasks by checking:

1. Both LEDs fade smoothly.
2. The two LEDs use their configured PWM frequencies.
3. The OLED initializes and displays the brightness percentages and frequencies in Task 2.
4. The Serial Monitor displays the PWM information.

Conclusion

Task 1 demonstrates independent PWM control and LED fading using the ESP32. Task 2 extends the project with an OLED display for real-time monitoring. Both tasks demonstrate PWM generation, I2C communication, and non-blocking timing using millis().
